Why: the integer-ratio theorem kills flank e whenever some d in ds has e|d and 8|(d/e).
  x=  16: dead flanks [2] ; alive [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 16] ; actually supporting [16]
  x=  32: dead flanks [2, 4] ; alive [3, 5, 6, 7, 32] ; actually supporting [32]
  x=  64: dead flanks [2, 4] ; alive [3, 5, 6, 7, 64] ; actually supporting [64]

Is the base {2,3,4,5,6,7} itself special? same test with other 6-element bases:
  base [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]     + [16,32,64] -> |S| = ['5', '4', '4']
  base [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]     + [16,32,64] -> |S| = ['1', '1', '1']
  base [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]     + [16,32,64] -> |S| = ['5', '4', '3']
  base [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11]    + [16,32,64] -> |S| = ['4', '4', '3']
  base [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13]   + [16,32,64] -> |S| = ['3', '3', '3']
